'''Energia d'Abyala, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Energia d'Abyala}}), commonly referred to as '''EAB''', is a Balboan energy commodity trading company and the second-largest commodity trader on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] after [[Recursos del Marañón]]. The company trades {{w|natural gas}}, {{w|refined petroleum products|refined products}}, {{w|liquefied natural gas|LNG}}, and {{w|electricity|power}} across Abyala and Lurandia, sourcing from producers including [[CIEA]] and [[PETROPOTO]] and selling to industrial buyers, power generators, and distribution companies.

EAB is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: EAB) and is a constituent of the [[SCX 30]] index. The company was founded in 1994 by [[Joan Ballester i Font]], a former [[SY Balboa]] investment banker who identified an opportunity to build a dedicated energy trading house as Abyala's petroleum and natural gas markets liberalised.

== History ==

=== Founding (1994) ===

[[Joan Ballester i Font]] founded Energia d'Abyala in 1994 after leaving the energy project finance desk at [[SY Balboa]], where he had spent six years financing oil and gas infrastructure projects across Abyala. During his tenure at SY Balboa, Ballester had arranged debt facilities for CIEA's Badia Curta refinery expansion, PETROPOTO's Marañón basin pipeline network, and several independent power projects in Sanrobonia and Tapuya. This work gave him an intimate understanding of Abyala's energy flows — who produced what, who bought what, and where the pricing inefficiencies lay.

Ballester recognised that the liberalisation of Abyala's energy markets in the early 1990s — driven by the privatisation of state oil companies, the unbundling of power sectors, and the gradual deregulation of natural gas distribution — was creating a demand for independent energy traders. [[Recursos del Marañón]] (RDM) dominated oil trading, but no Balboan company was focused on the rapidly growing natural gas and refined products markets. Ballester left SY Balboa with two colleagues and founded EAB with ƒ50 million in startup capital, partly provided by SY Balboa's venture arm, which took a small equity stake in the new company.

=== Growth and expansion (1995–2010) ===

EAB's early years were focused on natural gas trading. The company began by purchasing associated gas from CIEA's Abyalan operations — gas that would otherwise be flared — and selling it to industrial buyers and power generators in Balboa and Sanrobonia. This business was modest in volume but high in margin, as the gas was effectively a byproduct and EAB could price it below pipeline-imported alternatives.

The late 1990s and 2000s saw EAB expand into refined petroleum products trading, leveraging its relationship with CIEA to purchase gasoline, diesel, and jet fuel from the Badia Curta refinery complex and distribute them to markets across Abyala where CIEA's own distribution network did not reach. The company also began trading {{w|liquefied natural gas|LNG}}, purchasing cargoes from Sanrobonian liquefaction facilities and delivering them to import terminals in Lurandia, where natural gas demand was growing rapidly for power generation.

In 2003, EAB entered the power trading market, purchasing electricity from independent power producers in Tapuya and Sanrobonia and selling it to industrial buyers and distribution companies. The power trading business was initially small but grew significantly as Abyala's electricity markets continued to liberalise.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 2005, with the Ballester family retaining a controlling stake. The listing raised capital that was used to expand the company's trading desks and to establish offices in Sanrobonia, Potocsí, and Lurandia.

=== Recent developments (2010–present) ===

The 2010s saw EAB consolidate its position as the leading energy-focused commodity trader in Abyala. The company's natural gas trading business grew significantly as CIEA expanded its Abyalan production and as new LNG import terminals were built in Lurandia. The company also developed a substantial energy derivatives desk, offering {{w|hedging}} products to producers and consumers seeking to manage price exposure.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 produced a sharp decline in energy demand, and EAB's trading volumes fell significantly in the first half of the year. However, the company's diversified portfolio — spanning natural gas, refined products, LNG, and power — allowed it to weather the downturn better than more specialised traders. The recovery in energy demand in 2021 and 2022 produced record trading volumes, and the company's net income reached an all-time high in 2022.

By 2026, EAB employed approximately 3,500 people and operated trading offices in Sant Cristòfor, Sanropura, Port Soledat, and two Lurandian cities. The company is the second-largest commodity trader on the Bolsa after RDM and the largest dedicated energy trader in Abyala.

== Operations ==

EAB operates across four principal trading divisions:

* '''Natural gas trading''': The company's largest and most profitable business, trading natural gas sourced from CIEA's Abyalan operations and from independent producers in Sanrobonia and Potocsí. EAB sells gas to industrial buyers, power generators, and distribution companies across Abyala and Lurandia.
* '''Refined products trading''': EAB purchases gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, and other refined products from CIEA's Badia Curta refinery complex and distributes them to markets where CIEA's own distribution network is limited, particularly in interior Abyala and Lurandia.
* '''LNG trading''': The company purchases LNG cargoes from Sanrobonian liquefaction facilities and delivers them to import terminals in Lurandia, where natural gas demand for power generation has grown rapidly.
* '''Power trading''': EAB purchases electricity from independent power producers in Tapuya and Sanrobonia and sells it to industrial buyers and distribution companies, taking advantage of the gradual liberalisation of Abyala's electricity markets.

== Relationship with CIEA ==

EAB's relationship with [[CIEA]] is central to its business model. CIEA is EAB's largest single supplier, providing natural gas from its Abyalan production operations and refined products from the Badia Curta refinery complex. The relationship is structured through long-term purchase agreements rather than an equity stake, distinguishing EAB from [[Grup Tècnic Petrolí]] and [[Nutrició del Món]], in which CIEA holds strategic shareholdings.

The purchase agreements give EAB preferential access to CIEA's associated gas production — gas that is produced as a byproduct of oil extraction and would otherwise be flared or reinjected. EAB's ability to monetise this gas, which it purchases at a discount to pipeline gas, is a key competitive advantage and a significant source of margin.

The relationship is not exclusive. EAB also purchases natural gas from independent producers in Sanrobonia and Potocsí, and refined products from other refiners, to maintain a diversified supply base and to avoid excessive dependence on CIEA. However, CIEA's dominant position in Abyala's oil and gas industry means that the relationship will remain central to EAB's business.

== Ownership ==

EAB's ownership structure reflects its origins as a banker-founded startup and its subsequent growth into a publicly traded company:

* [[Ballester family]]: 22% — the founding family; [[Joan Ballester i Font]] serves as Chairman
* [[SY Balboa]]: 8% — retained from the 1994 venture investment
* [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]]: 5%
* Public float: ~65%

The company is professionally managed, with the CEO position held by [[Marta Riera i Soler]], a former energy analyst who joined EAB in 2003 and rose through the natural gas trading desk. Ballester remains Chairman but stepped back from day-to-day management in 2018.

